            <ArticleTitle>Interlaminar epidural steroid injections for low back pain in rural Ontario.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>95-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We sought to document the efficacy of interlaminar epidural steroid injections (ESIs) for the relief of low back pain in a rural population.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We conducted a prospective observational cohort study with brief follow-up telephone interviews at 1, 3 and 6 months after interlaminar ESI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 47 ESIs were administered to the 24 participants. In an intention-to-treat analysis, pain relief was achieved in 78.7%, 55.3% and 27.7% of participants at 1, 3 and 6 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Interlaminar ESIs, without fluoroscopic guidance, were effective for up to 3 months of symptom relief.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
